<h1>SDK Parity</h1>

<p>This page tracks API surface parity between the official Pilot Protocol SDKs for Python, Node.js, and Swift. It details feature availability in each SDK and the roadmap to close gaps.</p>

<h2>Status</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Node.js: Package `pilotprotocol` on npm. Feature complete. Includes TypeScript types, `using` support, and `Buffer` I/O. It is a reference surface alongside Python.</li>
  <li>Python: Package `pilotprotocol` on PyPI. Feature complete. Includes full type hints (`py.typed`), context managers, and snake_case naming.</li>
  <li>Swift: Package `sdk-swift` (SwiftPM). Supports core trust and datagrams. It uses an embedded daemon (XCFramework) with no separate process. Streams, networks, managed networks, policy, member tags, high-level services, and most registry admin are not yet exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Summary by feature category</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Lifecycle (construct, dispose): Supported in Node.js, Python, and Swift. Each SDK uses its native idiom (`using`, `with`, `deinit`).</li>
  <li>Daemon admin — `info`, `health`: Supported in Node.js, Python, and Swift.</li>
  <li>Daemon admin — `rotateKey`: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Trust — initiate handshake, list trusted peers: Supported in Node.js, Python, and Swift.</li>
  <li>Trust admin — approve/reject/pending/revoke: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Trust convenience — `waitForTrust`: Supported in Swift and Python (`wait_for_trust`). Planned for Node.js.</li>
  <li>Datagrams — `sendTo`, `recvFrom`: Supported in Node.js, Python, and Swift. Swift uses `send(to:port:data:)` and returns a typed `Datagram`.</li>
  <li>Datagrams — `broadcast`: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Streams — `dial`, `listen`, `Conn`, `Listener`: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Registry admin — hostname / visibility / deregister / tags / webhook: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Networks — list/join/leave/members/invite/respond: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Managed networks — status/forceCycle/reconcile: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Managed networks — score/rankings: Removed in v1.10; the symbols are no longer present in shipped libpilot builds.</li>
  <li>Policy — get/set: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>Member tags — get/set: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>High-level services — `sendMessage`, `sendFile`, `publishEvent`, `subscribeEvent`: Supported in Node.js and Python. Planned for Swift.</li>
  <li>FFI loader — `findLibrary` / `loadLibrary`: Supported in Node.js, private in Python, and not applicable to Swift. Swift embeds the library in an XCFramework.</li>
  <li>Typed response structs (`Config`, `StartResult`, `Datagram`, `Error`): A Swift-only idiom. Node.js and Python return untyped `Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;` or `dict[str, Any]` from the same RPCs.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What counts as a gap</h2>
<p>Naming differences across languages are not considered gaps. Idiomatic equivalents are collapsed into a single canonical feature.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Constructors: `new Driver()` (Node), `Driver()` (Python), and `Pilot.start(config)` (Swift) are equivalent operations.</li>
  <li>Cleanup: `driver.close()`, `pilot.stop()`, Python's `with`, and Node's `using` are equivalent teardown calls.</li>
  <li>Naming convention: Python's `send_message` maps to Node's `sendMessage`.</li>
  <li>Datagram receive: Node and Python return a `dict`, while Swift returns a typed `Datagram` struct. Both surface the same underlying RPC.</li>
</ul>
<p>A real gap is an operation that one SDK does not expose.</p>

<h2>Full method matrix</h2>
<p>The complete row-per-method matrix is generated by a deterministic script in the main protocol repo at `scripts/parity-audit/`. Re-running the script against newer SDK commits regenerates the matrix locally to the `./inventory/` directory.</p>

<h2>Roadmap</h2>
<p>The target is full parity across all three SDKs, except for the FFI loader and Swift's typed response structs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Swift: Gap-fill work is tracked to add streams, networks, managed networks, policy, member tags, registry admin, trust admin, and high-level services. The wire protocol support exists, but the Swift surface to expose it is missing.</li>
  <li>Node.js: Add `waitForTrust(peerId, timeoutMs)` — shipped in Swift and Python. Currently, Node.js users must poll `pendingHandshakes`.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cross-SDK versioning is documented in the GOVERNANCE file in the main protocol repo. All three SDKs share the same `MAJOR.MINOR` version line, with coordinated releases when the wire protocol changes.</p>

